From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Fill the fullest chunks first after a sweep

A sweep sorted each chunk's free runs onto the front of the bins in the
order the chunks happened to lie, so the chunks sorted in last supplied
allocation first whatever they held. What lived long was scattered over
chunks that were nearly empty, and none of them emptied enough to be
freed. In five hours the shell's heap went from 83 MB held around 41
live to 284 MB around 72, and of that 284, 135 MB was free slots on
pages still holding something live. The chunks a sweep keeps are now
sorted in by the slots they use, the fullest last, so what's made next
fills them, and the chunks using little are left to empty for a later
sweep to free.

That holds in every bin. A small request that no smaller run can serve
takes the head of the first large bin holding a run and bumps through
the rest, so the head should be the fullest chunk there, not the
emptiest. A request for a long run passes at most a few runs in the
last bin and may take a new chunk, but it fills at least half of it.
The kept chunks are put in that order where they lie, which the
partition before it already shuffles each sweep. The list the sort
uses is the allocator's, keeping its room from sweep to sweep, so a
sweep allocates nothing once it's grown. It's emptied before each sweep,
so it starts empty whatever came before, and after, so it holds no chunk
a later sweep frees. Ties go by address through
std::less, which orders any two pointers, so the order is the same from
sweep to sweep.

---
--- a/src/qml/memory/qv4mm.cpp
+++ b/src/qml/memory/qv4mm.cpp
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <chrono>
 #include <cstdlib>
+#include <functional>
 
 //#define MM_STATS
 
@@ -546,6 +547,30 @@ void BlockAllocator::sweep()
         return chunks.at(i)->sweep(engine);
     });
     const auto firstEmptyChunk = chunks.begin() + firstEmptyChunkPos;
+    // The kept chunks' runs go on the front of their bins in the order the chunks lie, so the
+    // chunk last in line is the one allocation fills first. Put in line by what they use, the
+    // fullest last, what's made next fills the chunks nearly full already, and the chunks using
+    // little are left to empty, for a later sweep to free, rather than each keeping a few of
+    // what's made and none ever freed. That holds in every bin: a small request no smaller run
+    // can serve takes the head of the first large bin holding a run and bumps through what's
+    // left of it, which should be the fullest chunk there, not the emptiest. A request of a
+    // long run passes at most a few runs too short for it in the last bin, and may take a new
+    // chunk, which it fills at least half of. The partition above already shuffles the chunks
+    // each sweep, so nothing counts on the order they lay in. The list is the allocator's,
+    // keeping its room from sweep to sweep, so a sweep allocates nothing once it has grown.
+    // It's emptied before, so it starts empty whatever came before, and after, so it holds no
+    // chunk a later sweep frees. Ties go by address through std::less, which orders any two
+    // pointers, so the order is the same from sweep to sweep.
+    byUse.clear();
+    for (auto kept = chunks.begin(); kept != firstEmptyChunk; ++kept)
+        byUse.emplace_back((*kept)->nUsedSlots(), *kept);
+    std::sort(byUse.begin(), byUse.end(), [](const auto &one, const auto &other) {
+        return one.first != other.first ? one.first < other.first
+                                        : std::less<Chunk *>{}(one.second, other.second);
+    });
+    std::transform(byUse.begin(), byUse.end(), chunks.begin(),
+                   [](const auto &byItsUse) { return byItsUse.second; });
+    byUse.clear();
 
     nextFree = nullptr;
     nFree = 0;
--- a/src/qml/memory/qv4mm_p.h
+++ b/src/qml/memory/qv4mm_p.h
@@ -230,6 +230,9 @@ struct BlockAllocator {
     ChunkAllocator *chunkAllocator;
     ExecutionEngine *engine;
     std::vector<Chunk *> chunks;
+    // The chunks a sweep keeps, by the slots each uses, which means nothing outside a sweep and
+    // is empty there; its room stays between sweeps so sorting allocates nothing once grown.
+    std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, Chunk *>> byUse;
     uint *allocationStats = nullptr;
 };
 
